"""ESP32-C5-MINI-1-N4: the radio, the MCU's debug probe and its data link (research/next-revision,
ESP32 radio and ESP32 form factor, A.6).

The module, U13 (Espressif ESP32-C5-MINI-1 & MINI-1U datasheet v1.4), flashes and debugs the MCU
over SWD (a CMSIS-DAP-over-TCP or Black Magic probe over Wi-Fi), carries the MCU's link (SPI2
half-duplex, UART4 as a fallback on the same pins) and relays Wi-Fi telemetry. The C5 is
Espressif's dual-band (2.4 and 5 GHz, 5180-5885 MHz, Table 7-7) Wi-Fi 6 part with a 240 MHz
RISC-V core (Features). The board has no USB: the radio flashes over its UART0 header J2 and over
the air, and the root sheet's SWD header SWD1 takes an ST-Link when the radio cannot reach the MCU.
Both headers take the programmer's 5 V pin as 5V_IN, which powers the board without the pack
(REGULATORS sheet, D17).

MINI-1 (PCB antenna) rather than MINI-1U (external antenna): every metal part of the robot is
18.1 mm or more from the antenna (drive blocks 18.1 mm, the left motor's can, past the centre
line, 23 mm, the M2 screw H4 25 mm, the LiPo pack 35 mm; MicrasHardware params.py,
spec/main2.toml [drive_blocks]), beyond Espressif's 15 mm clearance (ESP32-C5
Hardware Design Guidelines, Positioning a Module on a Base Board); the exception is the dielectric
floor 2.2-3 mm below. No published measurement quantifies that detuning, so the link is sized for
it: at 20 m, 15.5 dBm (the lowest 802.11a power, 54 Mbit/s, Table 7-8) arrives at -58.3 dBm at
5.825 GHz (73.8 dB free-space loss, 0 dBi antennas) against -90.5 dBm sensitivity at 12 Mbit/s
(Table 7-10): 32.2 dB of margin, 27.2 dB at 24 Mbit/s (research/next-revision/calculations/esp/
link_budget.py), so 10 dB of detuning and 15-20 dB of obstruction still leave the link at 12-24
Mbit/s PHY rates. The MINI-1 holds FCC ID 2AC7Z-ESPC5MINI1 and a CE (RED) EU-type certificate (Espressif
certificates page); the MINI-1U lists none.

Placement (spec/main2.toml [u13], [antenna_notch]): the rear-right corner, the module at 180
degrees with its 5.85 mm antenna area (Figures 10-1 and 11-1) over a notch cut out of the board
under it and beside it, its pads on the board. The antenna's ground point is at the module's pin-1
end (the pad-62 corner), its open, loaded end at the pin-45 end (Espressif's STEP model, parts
ANT-C5-MINI-1-top and -buttom; the datasheet v1.4 cover; the Hardware Design Guidelines' "GND
Point" figure on the ESP32-C5-WROOM-1's antenna, the same design mirrored): the pin-1 end faces
the right edge, 0.7 mm in (the keep-out figure's 2 mm at most), and the board is cut away 15 mm
beyond the open end (its minimum), the antenna end 0.5 mm inside the rear edge's line (its 1 mm at
most) (Hardware Design Guidelines, Positioning a Module on a Base Board, Keepout Zone for ESP32-C5
Module's Antenna). Keep the module away from the bridges, and dense GND vias along the module's
ground edge and the notch.

Supply (Table 6-2: 3.0-3.6 V, at least 0.6 A; 5 GHz TX peaks 426 mA, Table 6-5): 3V3_D from the
L6981C33 (1.5 A), with C109 22 uF and C108 100 nF beside the module's 3V3 pin (Figure 9-1: 22 uF +
0.1 uF) and C107 10 uF on its 3V3_D feed, off the antenna notch; the module decouples its chip
itself (Figure 8-1). 3V3_D carries about 104 uF in all, inside the buck's 30.5-145 uF window
(boards/main2/test/test_power.zen). The EPAD is on GND.

EN: R89 10 kOhm to 3V3_D and C110 1 uF to GND, the RC delay of section 9 ("usually R = 10 kOhm and
C = 1 uF"), so EN rises after 3V3_D (tSTBL 50 us, Table 4-8). J2 pin 1 pulls it to GND (reset).
Strapping pins (Tables 4-1 to 4-7):
- GPIO28 (ESP_BOOT): R90 10 kOhm to 3V3_D, the pull-up the schematic checklist recommends
  (Strapping Pins), with J2 pin 6 to GND for the download mode. No capacitor: the checklist leaves
  room for an unfitted one and warns that a large one enters the download mode.
- GPIO27 (weak pull-up, high: GPIO28 alone picks SPI boot or Joint Download Boot 0, Table 4-3;
  UART0 ROM messages per Table 4-5) and GPIO26 (read only with GPIO27 low) stay open.
- GPIO25 and MTDI (GPIO3), the SDIO slave's edges (Table 4-4), MTMS (GPIO2) and GPIO7 stay open:
  this board has no SDIO, and GPIO7 picks the JTAG source only once EFUSE_JTAG_SEL_ENABLE is
  burned (Table 4-7; with the default eFuses JTAG is the USB Serial/JTAG controller). The firmware
  enables the internal pull on every open pin (checklist, GPIO: high-impedance pins draw current).
Flashing header J2 (HC-PZ127-7.2L-2x3PS, 2x3 1.27 mm), wired as Espressif's ESP-Prog Program
interface (ESP-Prog user guide, Program Interface: 1 ESP_EN, 2 VDD, 3 ESP_TXD, 4 GND, 5 ESP_RXD,
6 ESP_IO0), so the ESP-Prog's 1.27 mm cable plugs straight in: 1 EN, 2 5V_IN, 3 TX0 (GPIO11, the
module's U0TXD, with the 499 Ohm series resistor the checklist's UART section asks for already in
the module, Figure 8-1 R2), 4 GND, 5 RX0 (GPIO12), 6 GPIO28. Pin 2 is the programmer's 5 V, into
VSYS through D17 (REGULATORS sheet): an ESP-Prog with its PROG PWR SEL jumper at 5 V, or any
USB-serial adapter's 5 V pin, runs the whole board without the pack; with the pack in, D17 blocks
it. A 3.3 V supply pin there leaves VSYS at about 3.0 V, under the L6981's 3.5 V minimum: the buck
may still start (its VCC rising threshold is 2.3-3.3 V, DS13554 Table 6) and run in dropout, 3V3_D
about 2.8-2.9 V, below the module's 3.0 V (Table 6-2), so the radio may not run; nothing is damaged.
3V3_D has no connector pin at all. Recovery: hold pin 6 low and pulse pin 1; the ROM's UART
download mode is in mask ROM, and only burned eFuses defeat it. Any USB-serial adapter with 3.3 V
logic does it (esptool's own reset sequence needs DTR/RTS on EN and GPIO28, as the ESP-Prog wires
them; with a plain adapter hold pin 6 to GND by hand and pulse pin 1). The header has no shroud:
pin 1 on the silkscreen sets the cable's way round.

Links to the MCU (A.6). The C5 pins are those the chip datasheet ranks free of any restriction
(ESP32-C5 Series Datasheet v1.5, section 2.3.5, priority 2: GPIO0, GPIO1, GPIO6, GPIO8-GPIO10,
GPIO23, GPIO24), none a strapping, pad-JTAG, UART0 or USB pin; their SPI2 and UART1 signals reach
them through the GPIO matrix (section 2.3.1). At reset each has its input and output off and no
pull (ESP32-C5 TRM v1.1 Table 8.13-1, Reset 0), and neither the datasheet nor the TRM lists a
power-up glitch on any pin (the ESP32-S3 datasheet's Table 2-2 has no C5 counterpart), so none
drives a line before the firmware does.
- SWD: GPIO0 to SWDIO (PA13); GPIO1 to SWCLK (PA14) through R86 22 Ohm (checklist, SPI: a series
  resistor on the clock trace).
- NRST: GPIO23 to Reset, driven open-drain by the firmware (NRST has its 30-50 kOhm pull-up, SW3
  and C21; DS13311 Table 61); high-Z until then, so the radio never holds the MCU in reset.
- SPI2 half-duplex, three wires (the MCU the master, the C5 its spi_slave_hd 3-wire slave):
  ESP_SCK from PA12 (SPI2_SCK) to GPIO10 through R95 22 Ohm at PA12, the driver's end (a source
  series resistor on the clock trace, checklist SPI; it is the TX resistor of UART4_TX in the
  fallback too); ESP_DATA between PB14 (SPI2_MISO pad, data line with IOSWP) and GPIO8 through R96
  220 Ohm at the C5: RM0468 Rev 3 Figure 643 note 3 asks for a series resistance on the shared data
  line against the two drivers fighting while the direction changes (the figure draws 1 kOhm), and
  220 Ohm holds that current to 3.346 V / 218 Ohm = 15.4 mA at most (3V3_D's top, R96 at -1 %),
  under the 20 mA per pin of DS13311 Table 11, before counting either driver's own resistance; the
  firmware turns the line round with both drivers off, so this is only a fault bound
  (firmware_changes.toml link). Its cost is an RC on the read direction, the C5 driving through
  R96 into the line and PB14: 220 Ohm x about 13 pF (CIO 5 pF, DS13311 Table 55; the C5's CIN 2 pF,
  module Table 6-3; about 6 pF of trace and pads, an estimate) = 2.9 ns, 3.5 ns to the MCU's
  0.7 VDD input high (Table 55) and 8.6 ns to settle (3 RC). At the /8 start (15.6 MHz: half a
  clock, 32 ns, from the C5's launch edge to the MCU's sampling edge in mode 0) that is 11 % of
  the half clock to VIH; at /4 (31.25 MHz, the fastest prescaler under the C5's 40 MHz slave
  limit, ESP32-C5 Series Datasheet v1.5 section 4.2.1.2) 22 % of its 16 ns, beside the C5's own
  GPIO-matrix delays, which the bit-error test the firmware runs before raising the clock covers
  (firmware_changes.toml link_spi); /2 (62.5 MHz) is above that limit. Writing, the MCU drives the
  line directly and R96 charges only the C5's pin (220 Ohm x about 3 pF, under 1 ns). ESP_CSn
  from PA11 (a GPIO) to GPIO9 through R88 100 Ohm, with R97 100 kOhm to 3V3_D at PA11 so the
  select idles high (no transaction) while neither end drives it, through resets and before the
  firmware runs; it is also UART4_RX's idle in the fallback. In the UART4 fallback the same pins
  carry UART4_TX (PA12) into GPIO10 (U1RXD) and the C5's U1TXD (GPIO9) into UART4_RX (PA11)
  through R88, the TX series resistor of the checklist's UART section.
- ESP_Flow: GPIO24 to PA9 (EXTI9), the C5's credit and attention line toward the MCU (PA9 has its
  pull-down: low until the C5 drives it).
"""

load(
    "./passives.zen",
    "C_100N_0402",
    "C_10U_0603",
    "C_1U_0402",
    "C_22U_0805_25V",
    "R_100K_0402",
    "R_100_0402",
    "R_10K_0402",
    "R_220_0402",
    "R_22_0402",
)

Capacitor = Module("@stdlib/generics/Capacitor.zen")
Resistor = Module("@stdlib/generics/Resistor.zen")
ESP32_C5_MINI_1 = Module("github.com/Team-Micras/MicrasPCB/components/espressif/ESP32-C5-MINI-1/ESP32-C5-MINI-1.zen")
HC_PZ127_2x3 = Module(
    "github.com/Team-Micras/MicrasPCB/components/hong-cheng/HC-PZ127-7.2L-2x3PS/HC-PZ127-7.2L-2x3PS.zen"
)

# Its own sheet in the board schematic (layout/main2.kicad_sch).
Project(name="Esp", path="layout", layout=False)

# A signal port takes the name of the board net it carries (the firmware's pin label) wherever
# that name is an identifier, so its sheet pin and hierarchical label match the root's label:
# KiCad's ERC flags labels whose names differ only in case (similar_labels).
V5V_IN = io(Power)
V3V3_D = io(Power)
GND = io(Ground)
SWDIO = io(Net)
SWCLK = io(Net)
Reset = io(Net)
ESP_SCK = io(Net)
ESP_CSn = io(Net)
ESP_DATA = io(Net)
ESP_Flow = io(Net)

ESP_EN = Net("ESP_EN")
ESP_BOOT = Net("ESP_BOOT")
ESP_TXD0 = Net("ESP_TXD0")
ESP_RXD0 = Net("ESP_RXD0")
ESP_SWCLK = Net("ESP_SWCLK")
ESP_SCK_IO = Net("ESP_SCK_IO")
ESP_CSN_IO = Net("ESP_CSn_IO")
ESP_DATA_IO = Net("ESP_DATA_IO")

NC = NotConnected

ESP32_C5_MINI_1(
    name="U13",
    V3V3=V3V3_D,
    GND=GND,
    EN=ESP_EN,
    IO0=SWDIO,
    IO1=ESP_SWCLK,
    IO2=NC(),
    IO3=NC(),
    IO4=NC(),
    IO5=NC(),
    IO6=NC(),
    IO7=NC(),
    IO8=ESP_DATA_IO,
    IO9=ESP_CSN_IO,
    IO10=ESP_SCK_IO,
    IO11=ESP_TXD0,
    IO12=ESP_RXD0,
    IO13=NC(),
    IO14=NC(),
    IO15=NC(),
    IO23=Reset,
    IO24=ESP_Flow,
    IO25=NC(),
    IO26=NC(),
    IO27=NC(),
    IO28=ESP_BOOT,
)

# Supply.
Capacitor(name="C107", P1=GND, P2=V3V3_D, **C_10U_0603)
Capacitor(name="C108", P1=GND, P2=V3V3_D, **C_100N_0402)
Capacitor(name="C109", P1=GND, P2=V3V3_D, **C_22U_0805_25V)

# EN and GPIO28: RC delay and pull-up.
Resistor(name="R89", P1=V3V3_D, P2=ESP_EN, **R_10K_0402)
Capacitor(name="C110", P1=GND, P2=ESP_EN, **C_1U_0402)
Resistor(name="R90", P1=V3V3_D, P2=ESP_BOOT, **R_10K_0402)

# Flashing header, ESP-Prog Program interface order.
HC_PZ127_2x3(
    name="J2",
    P1=ESP_EN,
    P2=V5V_IN,
    P3=ESP_TXD0,
    P4=GND,
    P5=ESP_RXD0,
    P6=ESP_BOOT,
)

# MCU links.
Resistor(name="R86", P1=ESP_SWCLK, P2=SWCLK, **R_22_0402)
# R95 sits at PA12 (the clock's driver), R96 and R88 at the C5.
Resistor(name="R95", P1=ESP_SCK_IO, P2=ESP_SCK, **R_22_0402)
Resistor(name="R96", P1=ESP_DATA_IO, P2=ESP_DATA, **R_220_0402)
Resistor(name="R88", P1=ESP_CSN_IO, P2=ESP_CSn, **R_100_0402)
Resistor(name="R97", P1=V3V3_D, P2=ESP_CSn, **R_100K_0402)

# Schematic placement hints for `micras-pcb sch --draft` (pcb evaluates and ignores this value).
SCHEMATIC_HINTS = {
    "blocks": [
        {"title": "EN RC delay and GPIO28 pull-up", "parts": ["R89", "C110", "R90"], "anchor": "R89"},
        {"title": "Flashing header J2 (ESP-Prog Program interface)", "parts": ["J2"], "anchor": "J2"},
    ],
}
